---

## Call to Action: Find the Patterns

#### In the JSON above, try to find:

#### 1. **One array** (look for **[ ]**)  
#### 2. **One object inside an object** (nested **{ }**)  
#### 3. **Two primitive values** (string / number / boolean)  
#### 4. **One place where an array contains objects** (a list of `{ ... }` items)

<details style="background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px;">
<summary style="font-size:1.4em; font-weight:bold;">🎨 Check your answers </summary>

<div style="margin-top:12px; margin-left:14px; margin-right:14px; margin-bottom:10px; font-size:13pt; line-height:145%;">

<div style="background:#ffffff; border-radius:10px; padding:12px; border:1px solid #ddd;">
  <div style="font-size:13pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Color Key</div>
  <div style="font-size:12pt; line-height:150%;">
    <span style="background:#fff2a8; padding:2px 6px; border-radius:6px;"><strong>Arrays</strong> [ ]</span>
    <span style="background:#d6f5d6; padding:2px 6px; border-radius:6px; margin-left:8px;"><strong>Objects</strong> { }</span>
    <span style="background:#ffd6e7; padding:2px 6px; border-radius:6px; margin-left:8px;"><strong>Primitive values</strong> (string/number/boolean)</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="margin-top:12px; font-size:12pt;">
  Below is the same JSON with highlighted examples. You don’t need to memorize this — use it to check your pattern-finding.
</div>

<div style="background:#111; color:#f1f1f1; border-radius:12px; padding:16px; font-size:14pt; line-height:1.35; overflow:auto; max-height:520px; white-space:pre; border:1px solid #222; margin-top:10px;">
{
  "app": <span style="background:#d6f5d6; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">{ ... }</span>,
  "location": <span style="background:#d6f5d6; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">{ ... }</span>,
  "current": {
    "temperature": <span style="background:#ffd6e7; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">72</span>,
    "condition": <span style="background:#ffd6e7; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">"Partly Cloudy"</span>,
    "wind": <span style="background:#d6f5d6; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">{ "speed_mph": 8, "direction": "NW", ... }</span>,
    "alerts": <span style="background:#fff2a8; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">[ ]</span>
  },
  "forecast": <span style="background:#fff2a8; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">[
    { "day": "Monday", "hourly": [ { ... }, { ... } ] },
    { "day": "Tuesday", "hourly": [ { ... }, { ... } ] }
  ]</span>,
  "meta": {
    "data_sources": <span style="background:#fff2a8; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">[
      { "name": "National Weather Service", "type": "government" },
      { "name": "Local Radar Blend", "type": "commercial" }
    ]</span>,
    "debug": {
      "cache_hit": <span style="background:#ffd6e7; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">true</span>,
      "latency_ms": <span style="background:#ffd6e7; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">182</span>,
      "notes": <span style="background:#fff2a8; color:#111; padding:1px 6px; border-radius:6px;">[ "Forecast normalized", "Alerts list empty", "Hourly sampled every 4 hours" ]</span>
    }
  }
}
</div>

<div style="background:#ffffff; border-radius:10px; padding:12px; margin-top:12px; border:1px solid #ddd;">
  <strong>What this means for code:</strong><br>
  Once you recognize the shape, you can decide:
  <ul style="margin-top:8px;">
    <li><strong>Objects</strong> → you access fields using dot notation (e.g., <code>data.location.city</code>)</li>
    <li><strong>Arrays</strong> → you usually loop through them (e.g., <code>for</code> loops) to display multiple items</li>
    <li><strong>Nested data</strong> → you combine both: access into objects and loop through arrays inside them</li>
  </ul>
</div>

</div>
</details>

---

## Why “seeing the shape” helps

#### When you can spot arrays, objects, and values, you can write code more confidently:

- #### You know **what can be looped over** (arrays)
- ####  You know **what can be accessed by name** (objects)
- #### You know **what values you can display** (strings / numbers / booleans)

#### Next, we’ll practice pulling out a few key fields and using them to build a small UI card.
MARKDOWN
